CprE 308 Project 1 
Repository: cpre308-sp26-mysh-Group22

Group Members
Ping Wu

Overview
This project implements a simplified UNIX shell called mysh written in C. The shell allows users to enter commands similar to a normal terminal and executes them using core operating system concepts such as process creation, execution, and file descriptor management.

The shell runs in a loop, reads user input, determines whether a command is built-in or external, and executes it accordingly.

Stages Completed

Stage 1 — REPL and Built-in Commands
A working shell loop was implemented that displays a prompt (mysh>), reads user input, and parses commands.

The following built-in commands are supported:

exit [n]: exits the shell with an optional status code, with validation using strtol
cd [dir]: changes the current directory, defaulting to $HOME if no argument is provided
pwd: prints the current working directory
pid: prints the shell’s process ID
ppid: prints the shell’s parent process ID

Stage 2 — External Command Execution
External commands are executed using fork(), execvp(), and waitpid(). The shell creates a child process to execute the command, while the parent waits for it to finish.

The shell prints the process ID before execution and the exit status after completion.

Stage 3 — I/O Redirection
Input and output redirection are supported and handled in the child process before executing the command. The implementation uses open() and dup2() to redirect file descriptors and close() to prevent file descriptor leaks.

The shell supports the following:

cmd > file for overwriting output
cmd >> file for appending output
cmd < file for input redirection
combinations of input and output redirection

Stage 4 — Pipes (Bonus)
A single pipe between two commands is implemented. The shell creates a pipe and forks two child processes. The first command writes to the pipe, and the second command reads from it.

The parent process closes both ends of the pipe and waits for both children to finish. This allows the output of one command to be used as input to another.

Known Limitations
This shell only supports a single pipe between two commands and does not support chaining multiple pipes. It does not include background execution, command history, advanced parsing, or quoted arguments.

How to Build and Run

To compile the program:
make
To run the shell:
./mysh
